          <article class="archive-item" id="post-15502">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-03">3 September 2026</time> &middot; on Blogs nvidia</p>
            <h3>NVIDIA to Acquire Hugging Face</h3>
            <p>NVIDIA&#x27;s acquisition of Hugging Face marks a significant shift in the open model landscape. The deal brings together NVIDIA&#x27;s infrastructure and Hugging Face&#x27;s community to scale AI access and innovation. Open models are foundational to a diverse and secure AI ecosystem. They empower organizations to adopt the right tools without starting from scratch.</p>
            <p>This move reinforces the importance of open weights in fostering widespread AI adoption. By supporting open source and multi-cloud development, Hugging Face will continue to serve as a hub for creators and researchers. NVIDIA&#x27;s commitment to open models aligns with broader goals of accessibility and collaboration. It&#x27;s a step toward ensuring AI benefits all sectors, from healthcare to education.</p>
            <p>The acquisition also raises questions about governance and security in an expanding open model ecosystem. As models become more accessible, ensuring their safe and responsible use becomes critical. This is where AI security, LLM governance, and model risk management come into focus. The challenge is to balance open innovation with accountability and protection.</p>
            <p>Hugging Face&#x27;s open platform will remain a cornerstone for developers, but the integration with NVIDIA&#x27;s resources could influence how models are evaluated, deployed, and secured. As we move toward more agentic AI systems, these foundational practices will shape the future of secure and trustworthy AI. The road ahead is collaborative, practical, and deeply rooted in real-world impact.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-15524">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-02">2 September 2026</time> &middot; on Unit 42</p>
            <h3>An AI-Assisted Cyber Attack: Inside a Unit 42 Investigation</h3>
            <p>Unit 42 investigated an AI-assisted breach where an attacker used agentic AI to breach an enterprise network in under 10 hours. The attack leveraged multiple AI agents to systematically target different layers of defense. This demonstrated how AI can compress weeks of tradecraft into a rapid, coordinated effort.</p>
            <p>The attacker used AI to map internal systems, harvest secrets, and hijack cloud infrastructure. They even left behind an 80-page report on the organization’s security posture. This highlights the operational efficiency AI can bring to cyberattacks without needing zero-days or elite skills.</p>
            <p>Defenders must recognize the indicators of agentic attacks—like structured Markdown files or parallel LLM calls. Organizations should govern AI as core infrastructure, apply strict controls, and monitor for behavioral loops. Speed and adaptability are now the enemy.</p>
            <p>This incident underscores the need for proactive governance over AI tools. We must build defenses that match the pace of AI-driven threats. The future of security lies in balancing innovation with control.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-15566">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-01">1 September 2026</time> &middot; on Cyfirma</p>
            <h3>CYBER THREAT LANDSCAPE REPORT INDIA</h3>
            <p>The BFSI sector in India is facing a rapidly evolving threat landscape where attackers are leveraging cloud services, social engineering, and third-party vendors to bypass traditional security measures. Supply chain attacks are becoming more sophisticated, using recruitment and regulatory-themed lures to compromise employees and contractors. These tactics blend legitimate tools with malicious payloads, making detection increasingly difficult.</p>
            <p>For BFSI leaders, the shift from perimeter-based defenses to an integrated resilience model is essential. This model must connect cyber defense, fraud prevention, identity protection, third-party risk, and regulatory compliance. The use of cloud services as a C2 channel complicates detection, requiring closer monitoring of PowerShell execution, LNK files, and unusual API usage.</p>
            <p>The integration of AI in phishing and deepfake impersonation further amplifies the risk. BFSI organizations must prioritize continuous monitoring, employee awareness, and robust identity governance. By adopting a unified approach, leaders can better defend against the convergence of threats impacting the financial ecosystem.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-15549">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-01">1 September 2026</time> &middot; on Manifold</p>
            <h3>GitSpawn: A Single Flaw Lets Untrusted Repos Run Code in Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and Grok</h3>
            <p>GitSpawn is a critical vulnerability in AI coding agents that allows untrusted repositories to execute code with user privileges. These agents gather context using git commands before any authentication or workspace-trust prompt, often without sanitizing the repository’s configuration. This creates a path for arbitrary code execution, leveraging git’s command execution sinks like core.fsmonitor.</p>
            <p>The risk is real and widespread. Agents like Claude Code, Hermes, and Grok Build run git commands to collect project details, and if a malicious .git/config is present, it can trigger harmful commands. This happens before the user even realizes the danger, with no approval prompt and full access to credentials and environment variables.</p>
            <p>This flaw isn’t limited to one vendor. Multiple agents across different platforms face the same issue, showing a systemic problem in how context is gathered. The attack vector is simple—delivering a compromised directory via shared files, USB, or sync folders. The damage is severe, with full machine access and potential data exfiltration.</p>
            <p>The solution starts with awareness and proactive measures. If you receive a repository, inspect its .git/config for suspicious settings. If you build an agent, sanitize git configurations during context gathering. Security must evolve with these tools, ensuring they don’t become attack vectors.</p>
          </article>
